                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Closest thing that comes to mind themewise is Tokyo Jungle. Not sure how someone didn't think of this sooner.  Rather then playing humans with ak47s you now have a choice of animals, such as ducks, bears and turtles. While still provided with a standard array of weapons the real fun comes from adding some crazy items into the mix, including pelican turrets and rabbit bombs.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O2AbMnUs7XI\nGameplay consists of clearing waves while huddled around the ammo pile. Stalks fly through at random intervals dropping boxes containg weapons, items and health packs. Every 5-10waves you are faced with a boss fight which didn't involve much more then outputting more damage, would like to see extra attack moves and such added in future. Currently the game contains 6 characters, 2 maps, 3 game modes and 3 difficulties. Alternate modes included penguin and kitten rescues, where you are tasked with taking groups of animals from A -> B.\n\nDid feel it was a little on the easy side, even on higher difficulty settings but I do play quite a few twinstick shooters. Would love to see online co-op added in future as for the now the game supports local co-op only. Can't say I had a chance to test it but would like to think enemies numbers scaled depending on players connected, supports upto 8!. Both Gamepad and keyboard+mouse were responsive but unable to be remapped. Devs have plans to add a bunch of weapons, enemies, levels and further tweak damage output. Solid base to work from, keen to see where it goes.\n\nIf looking for a stylish twinstick shooter that's a little outside the box, Nature's Zombie Apocalypse just might fit the bill. Would make a wonderful couch co-op game but probably a little short lived if intending on just playing solo (with the current content). Cheers folks. :)",
